@flexiarg math-formalization/replace-enumeration-with-structural-counting
@title Replace Enumeration with Structural Counting
@keywords enumeration, native_decide, decide, cardinality, kernel, exact-sequence, kernel, quotient, factorization, counting
@audience Lean provers, finite-algebra formalizers
@tone heuristic
@style pattern
@grade technique
@provenance slice-3 math-strict compression mining (2026-08-12): 4 quote-verified marks across 4 independent read transcripts and 3 distinct problems (b00J02 b97J01 b98A03)

! conclusion:
  When direct evaluation of a finite cardinality is too expensive or extends
  the trust base, replace enumeration with a structural factorization whose
  factors are already kernel-checkable.

  + context:
    A finite group, matrix family, or combinatorial type has a computable
    cardinality. `native_decide` or brute-force enumeration can produce the
    numeral quickly in ordinary execution, but the proof must remain within
    the kernel's accepted trust and resource budget.

  + IF:
    The object admits a homomorphism, quotient, filtration, orbit
    decomposition, short exact sequence, or ordered-basis parametrization
    whose fibers and image have simpler known sizes.

  + HOWEVER:
    Structural counting is not automatically cheaper. Constructing the map,
    proving surjectivity, and identifying every fiber can require more library
    infrastructure than a small kernel computation. First try kernel `decide`
    on genuinely small objects; use the structural route when enumeration
    blows the elaboration budget, scales exponentially, or introduces an
    unacceptable compiler axiom.

  + THEN:
    Find a map that decomposes the object into a known image and uniform
    fibers; prove the map's algebraic properties; compute the kernel/fiber and
    image sizes separately; combine them with a cardinality theorem; and make
    the final numeral follow from those proved factors rather than evaluator
    output.

  + BECAUSE:
    The proof kernel checks the algebraic decomposition rather than replaying
    a large search. The resulting proof explains the number, scales with the
    mathematics rather than the state space, and avoids making correctness
    depend on compiler-evaluated decision procedures.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Try kernel decide once and record its resource behavior.]
    next[Look for a quotient, kernel-image formula, orbit decomposition, or basis parametrization.]
    next[Prove each factor cardinality independently before multiplying them.]
